.TH SWAB 3
.SH NAME
swab \- swap bytes
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B void swab(const char *from, char *to, int nbytes);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Swab
copies
.I nbytes
bytes pointed to by
.I from
to the position pointed to by
.I to,
exchanging adjacent even and odd bytes.
It is useful for carrying binary data between
PDP11's and other machines.
.I Nbytes
should be even.
.PP
.B Note:
it is declared by no header \- v7 had none and C11 has no such routine \- so a
caller declares it itself.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/swab.c
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.B Rewritten over bytes, because there is no 16-bit unit on this machine.
.PP
v7 wrote this loop over
.BR "short *" ,
swapping the halves of a 16-bit word.
.B short
is an alias for
.B int
here \- one 48-bit word \- so the v7 loop would swap the halves of a
.I word
and mean nothing to any caller.
.PP
The routine still does what its name and its purpose say: it exchanges adjacent
.I bytes,
pairwise, which is what a program reading PDP-11 data needs.  An odd
.I nbytes
leaves the last byte alone.
